From 45ed25ceb4850fa16050f17d1c89ea39f06bab60 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kryxzort <virajslko@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 18:29:05 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 18/18] audio: play sound objects the way Wallpaper Engine does
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

CSound started every file of a sound object at once, at unity gain, and
ignored the object's volume, startsilent, mintime and maxtime: a scene
with a vocal and an instrumental mix of one song played both together, a
track behind a user property at 0 played at full level, and a "random"
object played all its files at once, once. Wallpaper Engine (2.8.485,
decompiled) does this instead, and so does CSound now:

- "sound" is a string (one file) or a list of files. Every start picks ONE
  of them uniformly at random (repeats allowed) and plays it alone.
- Each stream plays at volume² (WE squares the field; no clamp, so a
  value above 1 boosts). "volume" is a user setting like any other: a
  literal, a {user, value} binding or a {name, condition} one, so a
  property change reaches it live. The host driver multiplies each
  stream by its gain (AudioStream::setGain); the SDL driver folds it into
  its 0..128 volume.
- playbackmode "loop" (also when absent or unknown): one file loops
  natively; several play back to back, a new random pick as each ends.
  "random": a file, then a uniform [mintime, maxtime] s of silence
  (defaults 1 / 5 s) on the host's clock, then a new pick. "single":
  one random file, once.
- The schedule runs on the context's renderTime (CScene::renderFrame
  ticks the sound objects), so it follows the host's clock: a paused
  or slowed host pauses or slows it. A file's end is when the driver has
  mixed its last sample, not a duration read off the container (an mp3
  without a header estimates that wrong by seconds).
- A volume change applies at the next pull, without a ramp. A volume
  rising from 0 starts an object that isn't playing (never a "single"
  one); a playing object at volume 0 keeps running silently and its
  schedule freezes.
- startsilent: not started at load. Only a script's play() starts it,
  and LWE's SceneScript has no sound methods yet: CSound::play() is the
  entry point for when it does.
- WPENGINE_SOUND_SEED (an integer) seeds the picks and gaps: the same
  ones on every run.

A sound object keeps one stream slot in the driver for its whole life
(AudioDriver::replaceStream swaps the file playing in it; a null stream
plays nothing), so wp_context_audio_stream_count counts the sound objects
that can play, also one held at volume 0 at load: a host that opens its
output only for a scene with sound still hears that object when a
property raises it. A startsilent object has no slot until it is played.

Also: decodeFrame's leftover-packet counter was a function-level static
shared by every stream (one stream's count drove another's decoder,
which could send it an empty packet = drain, silencing it); it is per
stream now. The SDL callback no longer blocks in decodeFrame on an ended
stream's empty queue, and its removeStream takes the stream-list lock.

Not done: spatialization (WE pans a 2D scene's sound by the object's
position when "spatialization" is set; attenuation / mindistance only
matter then) — parsed by WE, ignored here. muteineditor, visible and
queuemode don't apply to sound objects in WE either.

Origin: ours
Why: liascape-player played scenes' sound objects all at once and ignored their volume and user properties (docs/player/spikes/we-sound-semantics.md § 4: 3289734539, 2951169110, 3276522107)
Status: candidate-for-upstream
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
